{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Behold, this day they offered.</b> Like ״הֵן יְשַׁלַּח אִישׁ אֶת אִשְׁתּוֹ״ (Jeremiah 3:1), ״הֵן יִשָּׂא אִישׁ בְּשַׂר קֹדֶשׁ״ (Haggai 2:12). Its meaning: if the matter were so — that these were offering their obligatory sin-offering and their voluntary burnt-offering, even though they are not communal offerings for the generations — then we would be eating the sin-offering today while we are אוֹנְנִים (mourners before burial)." "<b>Would it be pleasing in the eyes of HaShem?</b> That we eat קָדְשֵׁי דוֹרוֹת (offerings fixed for the generations) in אֲנִינוּת (pre-burial mourning). It is well known that the eating of an אוֹנֵן cannot effect atonement with proper intention, as it says regarding unconsecrated offerings (Deuteronomy 26:14): ״לֹא אָכַלְתִּי בְאֹנִי מִמֶּנּוּ״. And even though You commanded that we eat in אֲנִינוּת the meal-offering, which is קָדְשֵׁי שָׁעָה (offerings for that occasion only), this would not apply to קָדְשֵׁי דוֹרוֹת."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "Sforno's point is that Aaron's argument is a conditional: IF these were merely private offerings, then eating them even in אנינות would be in order."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:36:40.081894374Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}